          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What sort of dietary change does guidance describe for high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "The main dietary guidance for high blood pressure describes an overall eating pattern, not a single \"blood pressure food.\" Per the document \"Diet for high blood pressure,\" this pattern emphasizes:\n\n- Vegetables, fruit, and whole grains\n- Low-fat dairy, poultry, and fish\n- Beans, nuts, and vegetable oils\n\nIt also limits saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets, and sugary drinks. The same document says that adding one \"healthy\" food to an otherwise unchanged diet does not produce the same result, because the benefit comes from changing the whole pattern. It also says that cutting sodium (salt) combined with this pattern lowers blood pressure more than either change alone.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover specific portion sizes, daily sodium targets, or how this applies to any particular person. A doctor or dietitian can help work out what fits your situation.\n\nSources: \"Diet for high blood pressure.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
